course: history
lesson: 29
title: The age of science
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: For centuries people trusted old books to say how the world works; in the age of science they began to test ideas by looking, measuring and experimenting, and when the test disagreed with the book, the book lost.
explain: In 1543 Copernicus wrote that the Earth goes round the Sun, Galileo's telescope found moons circling Jupiter, and Newton showed that the same gravity pulls an apple down and keeps the Moon going round the Earth.
explain: Microscopes revealed tiny living things in a drop of water, and in the 1800s Pasteur and Koch proved that germs cause many diseases, which is why washing your hands before lunch saves lives.
explain: Jenner's vaccine against smallpox led to the disease being wiped out completely in 1980, Darwin explained how living things slowly change over many generations, and Marie Curie became the first person to win two Nobel Prizes.
fact: In 1543 Nicolaus Copernicus wrote that the Earth goes around the Sun, while most people before had believed the Sun went around the Earth.
fact: Galileo Galilei used a telescope to see moons around Jupiter and mountains on the Moon, and the Church put him on trial in 1633 and forced him to take back his support for Copernicus.
fact: The telescope was invented in the Netherlands in 1608, and Galileo built better ones and turned them to the sky.
fact: Isaac Newton explained that gravity makes an apple fall and keeps the Moon going around the Earth, and his laws of motion are still used to send rockets into space.
fact: Newton said a falling apple made him think about gravity, but the story that it hit him on the head is probably made up.
fact: The scientific method means testing ideas by careful experiments and measurements rather than trusting old books.
fact: Antonie van Leeuwenhoek made tiny powerful lenses and was the first to see living microbes in a drop of water, in the 1670s.
fact: William Harvey showed in 1628 that the heart pumps blood around the body in a circle.
fact: In the 1800s Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch proved that germs cause many diseases, and washing hands, clean water and pasteurised milk saved millions of lives.
fact: In 1796 Edward Jenner protected a boy from smallpox by giving him the much milder cowpox.
fact: Smallpox, which once killed millions, was wiped out completely in 1980 thanks to vaccines.
fact: Charles Darwin sailed on HMS Beagle to places such as the Galápagos Islands, where finches and tortoises differed from island to island, and explained how living things slowly change over many generations, by evolution.
fact: Marie Curie, born in Poland and working in France, discovered the radioactive elements polonium and radium and was the first person to win two Nobel Prizes, in two different sciences, physics in 1903 and chemistry in 1911.
exercise: What did most people believe before Copernicus? | That the Sun went around the Earth | That the Earth went around the Moon | That the Earth was the size of a city | That there was no Sun
exercise: In which year did Copernicus publish his idea? | 1543 | 1066 | 1969 | 1215
exercise: What did Galileo see through his telescope? | Moons around Jupiter and mountains on the Moon | Rings around the Earth made of ice and dust | Cities on Mars | The Sun going round the Earth every day and night
exercise: What did the Church do to Galileo? | Put him on trial and made him take back his ideas | Made him Pope | Gave him a gold medal and a big house in Rome to live in | Sent him to the Moon
exercise: What keeps the Moon going around the Earth? | Gravity | The wind | Magnetism from Jupiter | Nothing, it floats freely
exercise: Did an apple really hit Newton on the head? | Probably not, though a falling apple made him think | Yes, it fell right on his head and knocked him out cold | Yes, and it was a pear | No, he never saw an apple
exercise: What do scientists do to check an idea? | Test it with experiments and measurements | Look it up in an old book, believe it and stop there | Vote on it | Ask the king
exercise: What did Leeuwenhoek see in a drop of water? | Tiny living microbes | Fish eggs and sand only | Specks of gold dust from the river | Nothing at all
exercise: What did William Harvey discover? | The heart pumps blood around the body in a circle | The brain is full of air that cools the blood down all day | Blood stays still in the body | The heart is a kind of lung
exercise: Why do we wash our hands? | To wash away germs that cause disease | To make our hands softer and look nicer | To change their colour | Because Newton said so
exercise: Who proved that germs cause many diseases? | Pasteur and Koch | Newton and Galileo Galilei | Darwin and Captain Cook | Columbus and Magellan
exercise: How did Jenner protect a boy from smallpox? | By giving him the mild cowpox first | By feeding him lemons and oranges every day | By keeping him in the dark | By cutting his hair
exercise: Which disease was wiped out by vaccines in 1980? | Smallpox | The common cold | Scurvy at sea | Chickenpox
exercise: Where did Darwin study finches and tortoises? | The Galápagos Islands | Iceland | The Sahara desert in Africa | The frozen land of Antarctica
exercise: How many Nobel Prizes did Marie Curie win? | Two | Only one | None at all | Five or more
exercise: Where was Marie Curie born? | Poland | France, near Paris | England, near London | America
test: bro says the sun goes round the earth u can literally see it move. what's the real deal | The Earth spins and goes round the Sun, so the Sun only seems to move | He's right, the Sun goes round us, as anyone can see every single day | Both go round the Moon | Neither moves at all
test: Galileo saw moons going around Jupiter. Why did that upset the old idea that everything circles the Earth? | Here were things clearly circling something else | Jupiter's moons were circling the Earth, like everything | It proved Jupiter was flat | It had nothing to do with it
test: An old book says heavy things always fall faster. What would a scientist of this age do? | Drop things and measure what really happens | Believe the old book just because it is so very old | Ask the king to decide | Burn the book and guess
test: If smallpox no longer exists anywhere in the wild, why do we not vaccinate children against it today? | There is no smallpox left to catch | The vaccine was lost a long time ago | Smallpox is now completely harmless to children | Children cannot have vaccines
test: Newton's laws come from the 1600s. Why do rocket engineers still use them? | They describe motion and gravity very well | They are perfectly exact in every possible case | They are required by law | Rockets were invented by Newton
test: Darwin's finches on different islands had different beaks. What is the best explanation? | Over many generations each kind changed to suit its own food | Each bird grew a new beak in a week | Someone glued on new beaks | They all came from different planets far across space, long ago
test: A cut was left dirty in 1700 and a cut is washed clean today. Which is more likely to get infected, and who explained why? | The dirty one; Pasteur and Koch showed germs cause infection | The clean one; Newton showed water is dangerous | Both the same; germs do not exist | The clean one; Darwin showed soap causes disease
test: Marie Curie won Nobel Prizes in two different sciences. What does that show? | Great discoveries can come from anyone, and she was the first to win two | Prizes were given out to everybody who did science then | Women were only allowed one science | She bought the prizes
test: A cartoon shows Galileo inventing the telescope. What is more accurate? | Telescopes were invented in the Netherlands; he improved one and turned it to the sky | He invented telescopes and glasses | Newton gave him one | Telescopes came after the Moon landing
test: Why did seeing microbes help people later understand disease? | It showed a hidden world of tiny living things | It proved diseases come from the stars and the Moon | It showed water was poisonous | It had nothing to do with disease
test: Two friends argue about whose idea is right. What is the scientist's way to settle it? | Do a fair test and look at the results | The louder one wins | The older one wins | Flip a coin
exam: Identify the astronomer who in fifteen hundred and forty-three advanced the proposition that the Earth revolves about the Sun. | Nicolaus Copernicus | Isaac Newton | Charles Darwin | William Harvey
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the scientific method? | The testing of hypotheses by experiment and measurement | Deference to the authority of ancient texts | The settling of questions by majority vote | Reliance upon the opinion of rulers
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the disease of smallpox. | It was eradicated by vaccination in nineteen hundred and eighty | It remains common throughout the world | It was cured by the consumption of citrus fruit in great quantity | It was never a cause of death
exam: Ascertain the distinction first achieved by Marie Curie among the recipients of the Nobel Prize. | She was the first person to receive two such prizes | She was the first person to decline the prize | She was the first person born in France to receive it | She received the prize for literature
